  "response": "When an appellate court confronts a claim that expert testimony should never have reached the jury, it begins with the framework governing admissibility. Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals assigned trial judges a gatekeeping role over the admission of expert testimony. Although the decision entrusted judges with that screening function, Daubert held that the Frye general-acceptance test remains the exclusive standard for admitting scientific evidence. The appellate inquiry, however, does not end once a defect in the application of that standard is identified. A reviewing court must separate two distinct questions: whether the trial judge erred, and whether the error, once established, affected the outcome to a degree the law cannot tolerate. Conflating the two would convert every evidentiary misstep into an automatic retrial, a result the harmless-error doctrine exists to prevent.\n\nConsider a prosecution in which a firearms examiner testified that bullets recovered from the victim were fired from a particular pistol. The trial judge admitted the ballistics testimony without holding a Daubert hearing; that is, no Daubert hearing took place before the ballistics testimony was admitted. The record also shows that the trial judge admitted the ballistics expert's testimony over the defense's objection. That objection preserved the issue for review and spared the defendant the heavier burden of plain-error analysis. Once preservation is secured and the error is conceded or found, the appellate court sets aside the reliability of toolmark comparison as a discipline and turns instead to the consequences of the admission at this particular trial.\n\nThose consequences depend on what else the jury heard. The trial record supported at least one of two inferences, perhaps both: the defendant was present at the scene of the shooting, or the defendant supplied the weapon used in the shooting. The parties drew opposing lessons from that record. The prosecution argued that the ballistics expert's testimony had little effect on the jury's verdict, and because the prosecution pressed that position, the defense argued that the ballistics expert's testimony was central to the jury's verdict. Competing characterizations of this kind arise in nearly every harmless-error appeal, and a reviewing court does not resolve them by crediting the more forceful advocate.\n\nInstead, the court reconstructs the evidentiary landscape the jury actually confronted, examining the closing arguments, the instructions, and the weight each side placed on particular witnesses. The decisive question is structural, and it admits only two answers: either the ballistics expert's testimony was the only evidence linking the defendant to the shooting, or evidence other than the ballistics expert's testimony linked the defendant to the shooting. One of these must hold, and they cannot both be true. The measure the court applies to that question comes from constitutional doctrine. Chapman v. California held that some constitutional errors can be harmless if they are harmless beyond a reasonable doubt, a rule the Court articulated before the case was decided in 1973. Whether a particular evidentiary error rises to constitutional dimension varies by jurisdiction, but many courts borrow the Chapman formulation whenever the challenged testimony implicates confrontation or due process.\n\nThe allocation of proof under that formulation is fixed and does not shift with the parties' rhetoric. Under the Chapman standard, the prosecution bears the burden of showing that the error was harmless, and that allocation indicates that the appellate panel ultimately concluded that the properly admitted evidence of guilt was overwhelming. The panel's conclusion resulted from the independent proof, wholly outside the expert's account, that tied the defendant to the shooting. The court of appeals held that the erroneous admission of the ballistics expert's testimony was harmless. The method, then, is neither a tally of the parties' assertions nor a guess about the jurors' deliberations; it is a disciplined inquiry into whether the verdict would have been the same without the tainted testimony, answered by measuring what remains once that testimony is removed from the record.",
